    <exercises xml:id="exercises-simultaneous-inequalities">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-draw-four-inequalities">
            <statement><p>Draw <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge -1</m>, <m>x \lt 4</m> and <m>4x + 9y \le 36</m> on one set of axes, shading the unwanted sides, and label <m>R</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>R</m> has corners <m>(0, -1)</m>, <m>(4, -1)</m>, <m>(4, \tfrac{20}{9})</m> and <m>(0, 4)</m>. The edge on <m>x = 4</m> is dashed; the others are solid.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Draw <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = -1</m> and <m>4x + 9y = 36</m> solid (the signs include equality) and <m>x = 4</m> dashed. For <m>4x + 9y = 36</m> use <m>(0, 4)</m> and <m>(9, 0)</m>. Test <m>(0, 0)</m>: <m>0 \le 36</m> is true, so shade the side away from the origin. Shade left of <m>x = 0</m>, below <m>y = -1</m> and right of <m>x = 4</m>. On <m>x = 4</m>: <m>16 + 9y = 36</m>, so <m>y = \tfrac{20}{9}</m>. On <m>y = -1</m> the sloping line is at <m>x = 11.25</m>, beyond <m>x = 4</m>, so the bottom corners are <m>(0, -1)</m> and <m>(4, -1)</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-points-in-four-region">
            <statement><p>For the region in <xref ref="ex-draw-four-inequalities"/>, which of <m>(1, 1)</m>, <m>(4, 0)</m>, <m>(0, 4)</m> and <m>(2, 3)</m> are in <m>R</m>?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>(1, 1)</m>: yes. <m>(4, 0)</m>: no, it is on the dashed edge. <m>(0, 4)</m>: yes, on a solid edge (<m>36</m>). <m>(2, 3)</m>: yes (<m>35</m>).</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-small-region-points">
            <statement><p>Draw the region <m>x \ge 1</m>, <m>y \ge 1</m>, <m>2x + 7y \lt 14</m> and list its whole-number points.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>(1, 1)</m>, <m>(2, 1)</m> and <m>(3, 1)</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>With <m>y = 1</m>: <m>2x \lt 7</m>, so <m>x \lt 3.5</m>: <m>x = 1, 2, 3</m>. With <m>y = 2</m>: <m>2x \lt 0</m>, impossible with <m>x \ge 1</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-bakery">
            <statement><p>A bakery makes <m>x</m> loaves and <m>y</m> cakes a day. It can bake at most <m>40</m> items, must make at least <m>8</m> cakes, and makes at least three times as many loaves as cakes. Write three inequalities, draw <m>R</m> with <m>x</m> across from <m>0</m> to <m>40</m>, and count the whole-number combinations.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>x + y \le 40</m>, <m>y \ge 8</m>, <m>x \ge 3y</m>. <m>R</m> is the triangle with corners <m>(24, 8)</m>, <m>(32, 8)</m> and <m>(30, 10)</m>. There are <m>15</m> combinations.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>y = 8</m>: <m>x</m> from <m>24</m> to <m>32</m> (<m>9</m>). <m>y = 9</m>: <m>x</m> from <m>27</m> to <m>31</m> (<m>5</m>). <m>y = 10</m>: <m>x = 30</m> (<m>1</m>). Total <m>15</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-give-two-points">
            <statement><p>Give two whole-number points in the region of <xref ref="ex-small-region-points"/> and one whole-number point that is not in it, with a reason.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>For example <m>(1, 1)</m> and <m>(2, 1)</m> are in it; <m>(4, 1)</m> is not, because <m>2(4) + 7(1) = 15</m>, which is not less than <m>14</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-no-region-left-clear">
            <title>Challenge: An Empty Region</title>
            <statement><p>Draw <m>x \gt 3</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m> and <m>2x + 7y \le 4</m>. What do you notice?</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Where does <m>2x + 7y = 4</m> cross the <m>x</m>-axis?</p></hint>
            <answer><p>No region is left clear: no point satisfies all three.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>2x + 7y = 4</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>(2, 0)</m>. With <m>y \ge 0</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 4</m> needs <m>2x \le 4</m>, so <m>x \le 2</m>, which contradicts <m>x \gt 3</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-simultaneous-inequalities">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Use one colour of hatching for each inequality. Work <xref ref="example-finding-the-required-region"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-draw-four-inequalities"/> to <xref ref="ex-small-region-points"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-draw-four-inequalities"/> to <xref ref="ex-give-two-points"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-no-region-left-clear"/>. Extension: in <xref ref="example-vehicles-for-a-school-trip"/>, replace <m>m \le 10</m> by a tighter limit on matatus so that exactly <m>5</m> choices remain. (Answer: <m>m \le 7</m>, leaving <m>(2, 7)</m>, <m>(3, 4)</m>, <m>(3, 5)</m>, <m>(3, 6)</m>, <m>(3, 7)</m>.)</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Assessment levels</cell><cell>Exceeds: forms the inequalities, draws them and tests points on the edges. Meets: draws every inequality with the correct line style and shading and finds <m>R</m>. Approaches: draws the boundaries, but some shading or line styles are wrong. Below: a boundary is wrong.</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
</section>
